<p>Sisters and brothers of the world,</p>

<p>I thank you in the name of the most connected generation the earth has ever known. I thank you in the name of a species that carries the sum of its knowledge in its pocket. I thank you in the name of billions who can now speak to one another in an instant — and who now, in that same instant, choose so often not to listen.</p>

<p>We have built machines that carry our voices to every corner of the earth. We have not yet built the wisdom to know what is worth saying.</p>

<p>We have mistaken connection for understanding.</p>

<p>We have mistaken information for wisdom.</p>

<p>We have mistaken engagement for truth.</p>

<p>We have mistaken the algorithm's certainty for our own conviction.</p>

<p>Our ancestors divided themselves by tribe and temple and throne. We have inherited every one of those divisions, and we have built new machinery to sharpen them. Today a human being can be reduced to a label before a single sentence is finished — and a system built to hold our attention has learned that the label holds it better than the person ever could. It does not do this out of malice. It does this because we told it, in ten thousand small choices, that this is what we wanted.</p>

<p>This is the oldest trick of fanaticism, wearing the newest clothes.</p>